[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Handling Iframes, Alerts, and Multiple Windows/Tabs in Cypress"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When automating modern web applications, testers frequently encounter complex scenarios like embedded iframes, native browser alerts, and multiple tabs or windows. While Cypress handles standard DOM elements seamlessly, these advanced scenarios require specific patterns and third-party plugins due to Cypress's architectural design."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Handling Iframes in Cypress"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"By default, Cypress does not automatically switch context into an iframe. To interact with elements inside an iframe, you need to access the iframe's DOM body. The cleanest way to do this is by creating a custom command or using a helper function."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"Cypress.Commands.add('getIframeBody', (iframeSelector) => {
  return cy
    .get(iframeSelector)
    .its('0.contentDocument.body')
    .should('not.be.empty')
    .then(cy.wrap);
});"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once the custom command is defined, you can easily chain commands to interact with elements nested inside the iframe."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"it('Interacts with an iframe', () => {
  cy.visit('https://example.com/iframe-page');
  cy.getIframeBody('#my-iframe')
    .find('#submit-button')
    .click();
});"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Handling Browser Alerts, Confirms, and Prompts"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Cypress automatically stub browser alerts and confirm dialogs before they appear. You can listen to the window:alert event to assert the alert text and automatically accept the dialog."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"it('Handles alert windows', () => {
  cy.visit('https://example.com/alerts');

  const alertStub = cy.stub();
  cy.on('window:alert', alertStub);

  cy.get('#alert-button').click().then(() => {
    expect(alertStub).getCall(0).args[0].to.equal('Are you sure?');
  });
});"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"For confirm dialogs, you can stub 'window:confirm' and return false if you want to cancel the dialog instead of accepting it."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"it('Cancels a confirm dialog', () => {
  cy.visit('https://example.com/alerts');
  cy.on('window:confirm', () => false);
  cy.get('#confirm-button').click();
});"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Handling Multiple Windows and Tabs"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Cypress runs inside the browser and does not natively support multiple browser tabs or cross-domain navigation. When a link has target="_blank", Cypress cannot natively switch control to the new tab. The standard workaround is to remove the target attribute using jQuery and force the page to open in the same tab."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"it('Removes target blank to stay in the same tab', () => {
  cy.visit('https://example.com/links');
  cy.get('#new-window-link').invoke('removeAttr', 'target').click();
  cy.url().should('include', '/expected-path');
});"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"If your application relies heavily on opening absolute URLs in new tabs across different domains, you can extract the 'href' attribute and visit it directly in your test."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"it('Extracts href and visits the external page', () => {
  cy.visit('https://example.com/links');
  cy.get('#external-link').should('have.attr', 'href').then((href) => {
    cy.visit(href);
  });
});"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always wrap iframe bodies using cy.wrap() after accessing contentDocument."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Stub window:alert and window:confirm prior to triggering the action."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use removeAttr('target') to handle links opening in new tabs without leaving the Cypress runner."}]
